<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karlskirche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1716">1716</a>–<a href="/years/1737">1737</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karlskirche is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo">Saint Charles Borromeo</a>, built by Emperor Charles VI in fulfilment of a vow made during the plague of 1713. Designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-bernhard-fischer-von-erlach">Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach</a> and completed by his son Joseph Emanuel between 1716 and 1737, it combines a great dome with a classical portico and two columns modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-column">Trajan's Column</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, expressing imperial and Catholic claims.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karlskirche.jpg" alt="Karlskirche" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Ledl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karlskirche_Abendsonne_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1713, a year after the last major plague epidemic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, pledged to build a church dedicated to his namesake patron saint, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo">Charles Borromeo</a>, who was revered as a healer of plague sufferers and was a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. An architectural competition was announced, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-bernhard-fischer-von-erlach">Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach</a> won over competitors including Ferdinando Galli-Bibiena and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-lukas-von-hildebrandt">Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt</a>. Construction began in 1716 under the supervision of Anton Erhard Martinelli. After the elder Fischer's death in 1723, his son Joseph Emanuel Fischer von Erlach completed the building in 1737 with modifications to the original plans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The church stands on the Karlsplatz, on the border of Wieden and the Innere Stadt. The elder Fischer united very different elements. The façade leading to the porch corresponds to a Greek temple portico, and the two flanking columns, made by Lorenzo Mattielli, were inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-column">Trajan's Column</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Beside them, two tower pavilions show the influence of Roman Baroque architects such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini">Bernini</a> and Borromini. A dome rises above a high drum, which the younger Fischer shortened and partly altered. The church originally had a direct line of sight to the Hofburg and was the imperial patron parish church until 1918.</p><p>The programme of images came from the imperial official Carl Gustav Heraeus and ties Saint Charles Borromeo to his imperial benefactor. The pediment relief shows the cardinal virtues, with the patron saint at its apex. The columns carry spiral reliefs of scenes from the saint's life, and are meant to recall Boaz and Jachim, the two columns before the Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, as well as the Pillars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hercules-kassel">Hercules</a> and symbols of imperial power. Inside, the dome fresco by Johann Michael Rottmayr and Gaetano Fanti shows the intercession of Charles Borromeo, supported by the Virgin Mary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The high altarpiece of the saint's ascension was conceived by the elder Fischer and executed by Ferdinand Maxmilian Brokoff, and side chapels hold altar paintings by Daniel Gran, Sebastiano Ricci, Martino Altomonte and Jakob van Schuppen. The church is considered the elder Fischer's major work and an expression of the Austrian joie de vivre that followed the victorious end of the Turkish Wars.</p><p>The neighbouring Spitaler Gottesacker cemetery was the burial place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-vivaldi">Antonio Vivaldi</a> on 28 July 1741, although his grave has been lost, and the church holds regular Vivaldi concerts in his honour. Hedy Lamarr married Friedrich Mandl in its chapel on 10 August 1933. Since the restoration of the Karlsplatz as an architectural ensemble in the late 1980s the church has drawn attention for its dome and columns. It is cared for by the Knights of the Cross with the Red Star.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karlskirche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
